<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harappa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3300–1300 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harappa is an archaeological site in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, near a former course of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi">Ravi</a> River, the remains of a major city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. Settled from about 3300 BCE, it was at its largest in the Mature Harappan phase, about 2600–1900 BCE, covering some 150 hectares. The first site of the civilisation excavated by the Archaeological Survey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, it gave the civilisation its other name, Harappan. In 1857 railway builders took bricks from its ruins as ballast for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> line.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harappa.jpg" alt="Harappa" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Muhammad Bin Naveed · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_of_Granary_and_Great_Hall_on_Mound_F_-_Archaeological_site_of_Harappa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
